{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings","id":1127917,"title":"Players who hate Seekings","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-11T21:43:37-07:00","id":1127917,"title":"Players who hate Seekings","content":"Have any of you had players who angrily resisted the entire concept of Seekings? Have any of you been that player? I think Seekings are fantastic, but I have discovered that some people do not share my view. Apparently navel-gazing, random symbolism, and squooshy metaphysical exploration are not for everyone--not even everyone who otherwise likes Mage.<br /> <br /> For those of you who hate Seekings, could they be approached in a different way that would make you at least tolerate them? For those of you who have had players complain about Seekings, how have you dealt with it? Did you change your approach?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-11T22:09:17-07:00","id":1127921,"title":"","content":"Seekings are individual to the Mage and their Avatar, so I make the player come up with the majority of it and then I narrate them through it with a few twists and RP opportunities. This pretty much guarantees the seeking will be something the player enjoys, so I've not had a complaint. <br /> <br /> If I got this type of feedback from a player I would attempt understand why they didn't like this aspect of Mage. My immediate thought is that it must come from some misunderstanding of what a seeking actually means; and they may be limiting themselves to seekings that are of a Paradigm that conflicts with their character. Such as a player of a Virtual Adept who reads the part of the book on seekings and comes away thinking they must all be of a type that fits the mostly Hermetic viewpoint the book takes.<br /> <br /> If they truly don't believe that introspective reflection and pushing your boundaries, regardless of the form it takes, does not help one learn more about themselves and the world around them then I would begin to question their intellect.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12710-pazuzuaxelf","name":"PazuzuAxelf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-11T22:25:49-07:00","id":1127923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings#post1127917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have any of you had players who angrily resisted the entire concept of Seekings? Have any of you been that player? I think Seekings are fantastic, but I have discovered that some people do not share my view. Apparently navel-gazing, random symbolism, and squooshy metaphysical exploration are not for everyone--not even everyone who otherwise likes Mage.<br /> <br /> For those of you who hate Seekings, could they be approached in a different way that would make you at least tolerate them? For those of you who have had players complain about Seekings, how have you dealt with it? Did you change your approach?</div> </div> </div> </div>I am one of those players who hates seekings. I dislike symbolism and navel gazing, to me mage is a game about throwing magic and following a paradigm, with the latter serving as a self limitation i as player impose of myself in the name of flavor. They are always this very out of the way scenarios that i dont get and have to make choices whose impact i understand even less and 90% of the time it border the pixel bitching with the DM thinking they understand my PC inner working and missing the mark.<br /> <br /> As for introspection and reflection that to me is not something to force upon on a gaming table. I dont wanna do the equivalent of a one-one session and take the rest of the table as hostage to hear how my character masturbates his world view, nor i do like when that is forced upon me. Its it was a PBP thats another matter entirely as a player can just skip other\u00b4s seekings.<br /> <br /> What i do, is say it before the game start that i dislike them. So that the DM can just concentrate his energies and effort on the players who do like them and i take circumvent avatar (the merit that allows me to avoid them), if the DM is chill they allow me to take it for free. As a DM i just give it for free to every PC.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-11T22:41:15-07:00","id":1127927,"title":"","content":"I think it depends on the Seeking and the ST running it. I've had really fun really interesting Seekings and I've had godawful nonsensical Seekings. Not everyone is going to conceptualize a Seeking or its lessons the same way. One person sees meaningful and subtle symbolism, another sees shallow and pretentious bullshit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T04:35:21-07:00","id":1128004,"title":"","content":"Seekings are a strong conceptual element, but an utterly shit gaming element. Despite liking the idea, I tend to gloss over them in standard TT play for the following reasons in no particular order:<br /> <br /> 1) M20 seems to have moved away from this, but all the earlier editions of Mage had failed Seekings still cost XP. This makes failed Seekings entirely too unfun to have happen. Thus Seekings have no dramatic tension: you're always going to pass them. Even if you get rid of this rather stupid rule, then the stakes are still just when you can to spend XP on Arete. Basically, Seekings are ultimately boring to play regardless of how much you like the symbolism exploration because there's no dramatic stakes, and minimal mechanical stakes.<br /> <br /> 2) Seekings are inherently solo activities in a group activity. Seekings either have to be arrange outside of normal gaming time, or eat into normal gaming time while everyone else has little to nothing to do. This is a pain to manage in either direction.<br /> <br /> 3) The games continue to fail to give any sort of solid framework to Seekings. This vagueness in what a Seeking should entail beyond a loose set of common narrative elements means that the books do nothing to get players and STs on the same page. So what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2507_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2507\" data-vbnamecard=\"2507\">Caitiff Primogen</a> said here.<br /> <br /> 4) Perhaps a bit overlapping with the others, the time and energy investment on them in play is never worth the results. If the Seeking is relatively shallow, it doesn't take much to do, but you're not going to have much in the way of character shaking revelations happening either. Crafting an exquisite Seeking that causes a character to face themselves and take the risk of completely re-evaluating who they are is a ton of work... that's for a relatively small change in the game state. Yes another dot of Arete is a big deal conceptually, but it's not mechanically.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T08:02:27-07:00","id":1128047,"title":"","content":"I feel like Avatars were meant to run like Shadows in Wraith but with the reverse goal, but were never quite given the tools for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T09:11:58-07:00","id":1128069,"title":"","content":"I have run Seekings where the players spent the XP, failed the Seeking, and was out the XP. I know of others who have done the same (one is now one of my players). It's pretty harsh, but it happened. That was a long time ago and I'm not sure I'd do the same now, but I have done it. It's been a thing where the character was making a conscious choice on a different path than his or her bossy invisible &quot;friend&quot; a.k.a. her Avatar was demanding. The Avatar was still holding out some hope for the character so it didn't take away the ability to do magic, but was holding that out as a threat. It was all choices and role-playing and it was actually great. But then I've run and played in games where there were problems that really couldn't be resolved (a paladin who had to lose his paladinhood to do the right thing.... a superhero who died - and didn't come back, forcing the characters to deal with that in the kind of game where that never happens). It's even a factor that written into White Wolf games with the Dark Fate Flaw. <br /> <br /> Other times, the player(s) were often problematic in other ways, and, since the other players in the game had had to make hard choices and work and role-play hard to get through Seekings in which they were risking pretty much everything, it didn't seem fair to hand the recalcitrant player(s) an easy out. This usually was a sign post on the way to to logical conclusion of that sort of situation.<br /> <br /> I've had games where Seekings were something that players were all in for. They were the most intense and fun sessions of the game. Full-on individualized dreamscape realities or high-meaning-symbolic overlays on very magic rich in-game events with some more practical aspect. Ones where everyone but the other player whose character's Seeking it was would play some other part(s) in the overall passion play. Usually, the point wasn't &quot;win or lose&quot;, it was to determine what course of action the character would taking in the interval between Seekings. A path towards the goal. A lot of how that worked would depend on Essence - what was the next step on that path they were taking for Dynamic, what's the new path to explore for Questing, how they will continue to &quot;become&quot; whatever it is that they are intrinsically for Primal, what they are going to build on for Pattern. Since the players were all in, it wasn't like they were balky about figuring out their options.<br /> <br /> Currently, I'm running a game (which ends this month) where I've deleted Avatars all together. Mage is a game with some many working parts that sometimes it ends up just being too much noise to actually play out whatever type of game we're doing. Since this was a plot oriented game as opposed to a character oriented game, there wasn't going to be a lot of &quot;What is the is of is and how am I&quot; navel-gazing aspects so we just eliminated that to focus on what the characters were DOING not totally on who they were. To that end, dreamscape Seekings were pretty much thrown out with the talking marmosets, godly visitations or mysterious computer messages of Avatars. But I didn't totally get rid of Seekings conceptually. I drew from the idea of having a sort of &quot;Seeking mission&quot; that was set up when a dot of Arete was achieved and, with the expenditure of experience points, the character was faced with a critical problem to solve that would at least partially resolve whatever their chosen mission was. Then, of course, once that problem was solved, they would discover/set up a new goal that would be a core aspect of that charcter's personal story to be resolved with the next &quot;Seeking&quot;. In some ways, the Seeking was following the personal plot to it's resolution for the entire duration of the character having that dot of Arete. No dreamscape. No symbolism. No navel-gazing. It's a game about characters acting so the &quot;Seeking&quot; was just that.<br /> <br /> If a player hated the whole navel-gazing Seeking in a navel-gazing game, they could buy Circumspect Avatar and set up the Seeking as being something that presents itself as a more typical problem to solve and, upon solving it, they would have a &quot;Moment of Clarity&quot; that would indicate they just had a better handle on how to use their Will to change reality (or however it is that they see their magic working based on their Focus).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T11:38:49-07:00","id":1128107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am one of those players who hates seekings. I dislike symbolism and navel gazing, to me mage is a game about throwing magic and following a paradigm, with the latter serving as a self limitation i as player impose of myself in the name of flavor. They are always this very out of the way scenarios that i dont get and have to make choices whose impact i understand even less and 90% of the time it border the pixel bitching with the DM thinking they understand my PC inner working and missing the mark.<br /> <br /> As for introspection and reflection that to me is not something to force upon on a gaming table. I dont wanna do the equivalent of a one-one session and take the rest of the table as hostage to hear how my character masturbates his world view, nor i do like when that is forced upon me. Its it was a PBP thats another matter entirely as a player can just skip other\u00b4s seekings.<br /> <br /> What i do, is say it before the game start that i dislike them. So that the DM can just concentrate his energies and effort on the players who do like them and i take circumvent avatar (the merit that allows me to avoid them), if the DM is chill they allow me to take it for free. As a DM i just give it for free to every PC.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel your argument, and have a proposed solution. Outward Seekings.<br /> <br /> Much like Gilgamesh seeking immortality or Heracles seeking redemption, much like field scientists, you have to DO stuff, <i>real stuff</i>, to learn and follow your path to Ascencion.<br /> <br /> I can imagine practices where you don't get to decide &quot;I'm ready&quot; and then go on a introspective sequence where you get more enlightened. Many paths have to be followed constantly, the showing of enlightenment being something that happens somewhere along the road without a clear-cut aesop about personal growing: &quot;The Tao that can be named isn't Tao&quot;.<br /> <br /> With Outward Seekings, completing a &quot;Seeking&quot; would be much like gaining Willpower when you follow your Nature, but in a magical fashion: You always adhere to your path, and then, when you complete a task that you or your Avatar deem significative for your road to Ascencion, you get to buy the Arete point. You may have to follow vage omens to defeat a great darkness, or just build a great temple in the city to increase your merit (like some budhists do), you get a voice about what kind of things would furhter your path.<br /> <br /> If you want mage/avatar relationship, you may be given &quot;missions&quot; by your Avatar that can be as enigmatic or explicit as needed, but would always be something that you have to do in the real world. You can get all the help you need, but much like when following your Nature, you have to be able to say that it was a personal acomplishment. If you fail, you don't loose anything (but your actions, or inaction, may have consequences in the real world).<br /> <br /> Interestingly, with this method you may be able to thwart a rival's plan to get more enlightened, adding a new strategy to the War.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 08-12-2017, 02:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T13:06:30-07:00","id":1128139,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings?p=1128107#post1128107\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I feel your argument, and have a proposed solution. Outward Seekings.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is not a bad idea, as is pretty much in line with Circumspect Avatar merit.<br /> <br /> However to me is not so useful because i run revised era Ascension in which both the war is over and ascension is not as important. And i divided the game in 3 time skips in which the players cant raise arete until the end. So it would be an introduction all in Arete 2, a middle in arete 3 another timeskip to arete 4 and then the campaign ends. Makes handling the power level easier.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-12T13:17:54-07:00","id":1128152,"title":"","content":"I tend to work seekings into plot hooks and run them less as a pass/fail sort of situation but more like a rank challenge in werewolf where you have to do some task and you gain Arete","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-13T02:26:23-07:00","id":1128369,"title":"","content":"I'm going to run Seekings that are collaborative and involve the other players. Outward, as discussed above.<br /> <br /> The Dreamspeaker wakes up, and knows that their Avatar wants them to work to unite two warring tribes of Shifters. The Virtual Adept has run into rumours on a forum that a really rare antique PC (only 100 were ever made) was spotted in his area, and he gets this feeling that he could learn something from studying it. The Chorister has a religious experience, and feels like they need to recover a relic that has fallen into the hands of infernalists.<br /> <br /> And in every case these Mages feel the need to call upon the help of their allies. The ST should tailor the Seeking to challenge the Mage's understanding of their own paradigm, and the mastery of the Spheres they have learned. Multiple Mages could even share a Seeking, each taking their own lesson from it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-13T09:35:23-07:00","id":1128490,"title":"","content":"Both of the above encompass what we did in my no-Avatar game, which was fundamentally grounded in some serious time travel, cause/effect issues. Each of the characters was pulled from a different era and had amnesia about what was going on the moment they were grabbed from, though all those moments looked like they could have been fatal if they hadn't been gacked through time (one character was gunshot and bleeding, another was soaking wet and drowning, a third came though coughing and under a wet blanket, and so on). They gradually discovered they were all related to one another as well. And that there was some all-encompassing Plan they were supposed to fulfill. Typically, we hold off Seekings untl enough XP has been gained so the character gets not only the increase in Arete but also has the XP to blow on getting their &quot;signature&quot; Sphere up a dot as well. (The player on the Seeking is also getting the chance at some additional XP as well). The Seekings would tie into some part they were supposed to play in the Plan (which had something to do with saving the world from a future apocalyptic event), some important aspect of their past that needed resolving, and dealing with whatever path they had been on and how that was going to change as they increased in enlightenment.<br /> <br /> Example:<br /> Adam - Hermetic, Spirit from the turn of the century (19th to 20th) - He had been sort of a rootless, wandering, (somewhat depressing), rainmaker among the communities in and around Oklahoma, northern Texas, Kansas and the like. Originally, was an apprentice in House Tharsis when it was done away with, leaving him to be retrained under the watchful eyes of the Quaesitori. He uncovered that he had struggled with a very powerful air elemental who was causing massive storms resulting in a tornado that had largely leveled a town. <br /> <br /> In the town, he had to maneuver through politics with the local changelings and, eventually, wraiths (which required a lot of help from the other PCs, he's not the social type at all), uncover what happened and make sure it didn't happen again (the elemental was bound in slumber, there were changelings who thought that waking it up would be to their advantage...). To do so, he had to draw upon the House Tharsis magics that he had been forbidden to use, discovering in himself that he refused the limitations he had labored under for crimes he never committed and steeling him with a desire to see those wrongs righted and, as a long term goal, re-establish his former House. (Grand aspirations indeed.... That plan was definitely one that would carry him as his Seeking from 4 to 5 and probably 5 to 6, with a different role with different choices to make for each Seeking). <br /> <br /> As for the role in the Plan, apparently, somewhere down the line, some new Hermetic House, revitalized from the remnants of House Tharsis, (or, possibly, a new Disparate group or, even, conceivably based on later events, a Methodology withing the Syndicate) was going to be needed to do something that would assist in averting the apocalypse. <br /> <br /> There were four other members of the group who all had their own particular situations to deal with:<br /> - Rory Verbena, Life, bootlegger from the '20s, who had to go home and deal with some nasty leftovers from the dissolution of his family... while in a Jhor Quiet. Adam was his absent baby-daddy, so that definitely worked in)<br /> - Norman Virtual Adept, Data, one of the Virtual Adepts who rebelled against the Union in the '50s, who had a disconnect with the person he seemed to be and what he remembered (which seemed to end well before the VA defection when he was adissatisfied Technocrat), who had to resolve the issue of his missing chunk of his past and come to terms with what that meant for the person he was currently vs. the one he used to be (involving four kids and a wife he knew nothing about). The resolution on that one got REALLY weird. (Oh and Norman is Adam's grandson via Rory's twin sister.... I ended up using an online geneaology program to keep track of it all...)<br /> - Hank Ecstatic, Time, Transcendalist poet/Civil War soldier (for the Union/North), who is about as footloose and fancy free as it's imaginable to be. But various versions of his future self would pop up, mostly proximately, and, apparently, help him and his group along with the Plan. The &quot;moment of decision&quot; involves a choice to take up the mantle of making the choices that will allow for events to unfold that result in his future selves that have already helped him out coming into existence but having to give up some (a lot? all?) of that &quot;fancy free&quot; or basically throwing the underpinnings of the whole Plan (which might just be HIS Plan?) in extreme jeapordy. The crux of the matter is that Hank may have tangentially encountered future selves from both sides of that decision....<br /> - Douglas NWO, Entropy, from the era of the Cuban Missile Crisis.... well, you get the idea.... Suffice it to say, he ends up actually getting a grasp on the Plan, which is a squirmy, almost living thing (kinda like Aasimov's psychohistory, only manipulating the past to create certain future events) that requires Entropy 4 to really grok....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-13T12:15:28-07:00","id":1128537,"title":"","content":"Seekings can be either a great story element or an absolutely terrible one. They are not an element that fits into a gaming mindset, so it's natural that some people who really want to unite narrative and story in their games find them lacking.<br /> <br /> I've also found that Seekings are an easy way for a bad / naive ST to bully players about their characters. It's not necessarily deliberate, but it's a natural trap. Part of a Seeking involves the ST having insight into the PC, so it can devolve into the player wrestling with a incorrect perception of their character.<br /> <br /> Conversely, in the hands of a skilled ST, a Seeking is a great way to guide a PC to a more interesting story. Mage is a game of personal growth, and the Seeking is the primary method of generating that change in PCs.<br /> <br /> One of my buddies often says Seekings and other similar WW elements are a game of &quot;Guess exactly what the ST was thinking.&quot; In some cases, that's a valid critique. The key, I think, is for the ST to be a keen observer of the character, and build Seekings around them rather than the reverse.<br /> <br /> Also, Arete is expensive, and I've never participated in a game where a PC was stripped of XP as a result of a Seeking. The worst outcome was someone was denied the opportunity to buy Arete at that moment. They ended up channeling it into another Sphere and ultimately had more fun with that. If you're handing out less than 4 XP a session and you strip a PC of 40, that's borderline a dick move in my book. YMMV.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6601-totentanz","name":"Totentanz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6601_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-13T17:35:11-07:00","id":1128636,"title":"","content":"The criticism that makes the most sense to me is that it can look like the ST is claiming more authority of the character than the player has, through the device of the Avatar. I like what PazuzuAxelf and other shave suggested, where the player and ST discuss it ahead of time. That way it doesn't seem like a test where you don't know the rules. I don't really like the idea of it being a pass/fail thing--but I'm okay with it having consequences in the way the character's magic manifests. Maybe even mechanical effects where certain effects become harder and others become easier, as the paradigm shifts slightly.<br /> <br /> I am curious as to how long other ST's make their Seekings. Mine are like 15-30 minutes. Longer than Luke in the cave on Degobah, shorter than the longer productions of A Christmas Carol. LokiRavenSpeak, I don't blame you for disliking Seekings if they take an entire multi-hour game session. That sounds challenging to do well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-13T19:15:54-07:00","id":1128669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings?p=1128636#post1128636\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am curious as to how long other ST's make their Seekings. Mine are like 15-30 minutes. Longer than Luke in the cave on Degobah, shorter than the longer productions of A Christmas Carol. LokiRavenSpeak, I don't blame you for disliking Seekings if they take an entire multi-hour game session. That sounds challenging to do well.</div> </div> </div> </div>The last seeking i played with was one in which all 3 pcs were into and lasted 2 sessions. Its was well done however, i still didnt like it for the aforementioned reasons so i know i will never like them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-15T11:53:12-07:00","id":1129335,"title":"","content":"One of the other pitfalls of Seekings, and one that I suspect has soured many a player on the subject, is that STs often run them as challenges (riddles, puzzles, etc) for the Players rather than challenges for the characters. To be fair that's a common ST mistake in many situations, but it is particularly problematic here. Seekings are supposed to be integral and defining parts of a character and as such they should be things the player has an overwhelming amount of input on. That's why my preferred method is to merely facilitate players describing the Seekings themselves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-15T12:56:50-07:00","id":1129371,"title":"","content":"How does that work? <br /> <br /> I'm seeing everyone sitting around the table, while the one player narrates a personal novella starting their character with the ST playing the part of a Rogerian therapist making the occasional affirmative noise, saying &quot;How does that make your character feel?&quot; and &quot;So, what do you want to have happen next for your character, &lt;insert character name&gt;?&quot;. With the rest of the players acting out the part of the audience of a performance theater piece. <br /> <br /> Meaning, not much fun for anyone except for the player on the Seeking and then only for a particular type of person. It seems more like therapy, telling a bedtime story or pitching a script than RPing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-15T17:51:13-07:00","id":1129505,"title":"","content":"Well, something I've been part of that's along the lines of what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> posted that takes advantage of digital technology:<br /> <br /> The player writes up their Seeking, and the ST acts more like an editor (esp. content over copy in that regard). So yes, things like, &quot;how does that make your character feel?&quot; might be part of the redlining process, but you'd see far more, &quot;how does that impact your character's views?&quot; or whatever. The final product would go into a shared GoogleDoc that the other players can read at their leisure.<br /> <br /> It's not something I think would work for everyone (I know a lot of people that don't like doing solo writing things like that), but it's actually a pretty good way for player-facing Seeking construction that doesn't pull down group-time play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-16T07:16:22-07:00","id":1129771,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings?p=1129505#post1129505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, something I've been part of that's along the lines of what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> posted that takes advantage of digital technology:<br /> <br /> The player writes up their Seeking, and the ST acts more like an editor (esp. content over copy in that regard). So yes, things like, &quot;how does that make your character feel?&quot; might be part of the redlining process, but you'd see far more, &quot;how does that impact your character's views?&quot; or whatever. The final product would go into a shared GoogleDoc that the other players can read at their leisure.<br /> <br /> It's not something I think would work for everyone (I know a lot of people that don't like doing solo writing things like that), but it's actually a pretty good way for player-facing Seeking construction that doesn't pull down group-time play.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's exactly what I am talking about. You can do it in solo RP sessions too, with the Player narrating and the ST asking hard questions like 'why is this a harder decision than it seems?' or 'what's the lesson here your character does not want to learn?' In a group session the other players could potentially ask those kind of hard questions too, as well as offer suggestions and symbolism. There's actually a lot of leeway in what you can throw into a seeking provided the key player is the final arbiter of what it means and what the character takes out of it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-16T10:22:46-07:00","id":1129863,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings?p=1129505#post1129505\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, something I've been part of that's along the lines of what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"758\" data-vbnamecard=\"758\">Ramnesis</a> posted that takes advantage of digital technology:<br /> <br /> The player writes up their Seeking, and the ST acts more like an editor (esp. content over copy in that regard). So yes, things like, &quot;how does that make your character feel?&quot; might be part of the redlining process, but you'd see far more, &quot;how does that impact your character's views?&quot; or whatever. The final product would go into a shared GoogleDoc that the other players can read at their leisure.<br /> <br /> It's not something I think would work for everyone (I know a lot of people that don't like doing solo writing things like that), but it's actually a pretty good way for player-facing Seeking construction that doesn't pull down group-time play.</div> </div> </div> </div>That can work. I've seen it done in other games with a similar &quot;power-up&quot; structure. But, like you said, it doesn't work for a lot of people. A LOT of people. For any number of reasons: <br /> <br /> They aren't writers and don't want to be. That's not fun. <br /> They are writers but this is not their day job and having to write MORE for &quot;fun&quot;, isn't. (I've seen this happen to others as well. Say, accountants who are put in charge of something related to numbers and calculations and end up not playing anymore when everyone wants the &quot;expert&quot; to handle their day job &quot;specialty&quot;).<br /> They are or aren't writers but have very very full lives and can spare the time to game, but not to write a stream of consciousness ontological epiphany for something they have to work hard to set aside four or so hoursJUST TO PLAY once every two weeks or so...<br /> <br /> And so on...<br /> <br /> If it doesn't happen in game, it's hard to keep it even across the board for all participants. If that's what a Seeking is and I can't manage it, does it mean my character doesn't gain Arete? Is it given to me without having to do the Seeking just to keep the game going? <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-16T13:06:32-07:00","id":1129941,"title":"","content":"Dude, tone it back a bit?<br /> <br /> You asked, &quot;how does that work?&quot; I gave an example of a method that can work for some groups to give you something to picture that's closer to what's being suggested instead of your initial conception. I wasn't saying my example should be how Seekings are done, or that it should work for everyone (since, you know, I openly acknowledged it doesn't already).<br /> <br /> If you seriously want to discuss ways to actualize this approach to Seekings for different players, we can do that. If you want to shout about how my example is perfect for everyone, I'm not sure why you'd expect me to answer your questions or respond to your points..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-17T06:23:10-07:00","id":1130226,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1127917-players-who-hate-seekings?p=1129941#post1129941\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dude, tone it back a bit?<br /> <br /> You asked, &quot;how does that work?&quot; I gave an example of a method that can work for some groups to give you something to picture that's closer to what's being suggested instead of your initial conception. I wasn't saying my example should be how Seekings are done, or that it should work for everyone (since, you know, I openly acknowledged it doesn't already).<br /> <br /> If you seriously want to discuss ways to actualize this approach to Seekings for different players, we can do that. If you want to shout about how my example is perfect for everyone, I'm not sure why you'd expect me to answer your questions or respond to your points..</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry the tone came across the way it did. I know you definitely couched your example in &quot;not that this works all the time&quot; and I wasn't dismissing it out of hand. It's definitely a solution and one I've seen work for some players in some games (including me in a Scion game to an extent).<br /> <br /> I was throwing the issues I rasied out there because they have been the EXACT problems I have had with trying to handle Seekings (as well as other things that are more individual than group, like character creation) &quot;off camera&quot; (literally in the case of online games... which all of mine currently are). Juxtaposed with handing them &quot;on camera&quot; but having the focus devolve to just one individual (with all of the problems that that entails).<br /> <br /> I agree that players get out of a game what they can put into it, but, as I get older (and my group does too), there are players who simply can't put in as much as others or as much as they'd like to. And when the situation in question is important (and, with Arete gain and Seekings, core) to the game, that is a problem. Attached to the problem of the people for whom Seekings, in whatever format is used, are just not their thing. They are different side of the same polyhedron. And I have tried several different ways to manage it.... from the whole session dreamscape, reverse-Harrowing Seekings where we all pitch in and give each player their turn as the focus of the game, to minimizing the &quot;noise&quot; around Avatars and Seekings and integrating Arete gain with accomplishing an in-game task that results in a tangible gain in understanding of their magic and a commensurate gain in power (the &quot;Outward Seeking&quot; from above, or, &quot;Everyone Has Circumspect Avatar&quot; [for free]).<br /> <br /> But I don't consider the problem &quot;solved&quot; for us. And, after a break from Mage when we finish the current game this Sunday (YAY! and &lt;sniffle&gt;), the next Mage game is going to be an Awakenings game and Seekings are going to be on the table heavy-duty as the plan is for the characters to be Orphans building their own Foci with low level starting Arete and commensurate gains as they start to &quot;get it&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-26T20:38:03-07:00","id":1134345,"title":"","content":"My group is uneasy with the idea of Seekings so we've tried alternatives. To me, the key elements of a seeking are interfacing with the avatar and self-improvement. My ancillary goals were to include all the other players and either further the story or give a glimpse of another world.<br /> <br /> Methods will vary depending on your view of the Avatar. Our group agrees that the Avatar may or may not be a soul but Avatars seem to be independent and are essential if not responsible for the ability to do Magic.<br /> <br /> <br /> What we've tried:<br /> *Avatar challenge - The avatar issues a challenge to the character that needs to be maintained during a story. If the avatar is advancing metaphysical advancement this could be barring access to an overused sphere or maybe not letting a character use higher dots of a sphere. I have a Chorister who's overly fond of &quot;divine providence&quot; in the form of Entropy and Time and he's restricted to each at level 1 until the end of the current story to get a fourth dot of Arete.<br /> <br /> *Avatar quest - The player is given a kind of mission from their avatar. Finish business from a past live, carry out an activity that's particularly in line with one's essence, or simply a large undertaking to advance the avatar's agenda. This is similar to the symbol quest of a traditional Seeking but the cabal can cooperate. In a symbolic seeking a character may need to protect a unicorn as a symbol of purity but in an Avatar quest, the character need to protect an actual unicorn that's under threat. These take one session and can cover multiple Seekings.<br /> <br /> *Avatar session - Avatars may have a life of their own when a character is sleeping. In an Avatar session, the players roleplay their Avatar doing Avatar stuff. The one we tried was the Avatar as an element in an Umbral Court. The Avatar settled a score and gained power. The other characters also participated as their Avatars. This makes Arete a reflection of the power of their Avatar moreso than wisdom.<br /> <br /> *Flashbacks - This involves a character revising a choice that the character made that had large consequences which seemed unavoidable. One character has a personal ban against using life magic destructively due to the circumstances of their awakening. In one session for an hour we replayed the awakening and the character tried to find an alternative. It took two tries but the character found another way. The other players took on the roles of other characters present in the Awakening.<br /> <br /> What we haven't tried:<br /> *Analog to Vampire's Humanity - As the Mage gains more understanding their rule of magical ethics become stricter. The character must act in accordance with some metaphysical ethic to advance understanding. This isn't the same as a moral straight jacket but may be something like expanding ones tools of magic, investigating new paradigms and the like. We've not really found something we like with this method and failure would imply that arete could drop which seems terrifying.<br /> <br /> *Teaching - This may not make immediate sense, but to raise one's arete one must shepard and teach another Mage to their level of Arete. I often don't understand something until I teach someone.<br /> <br /> Avatar challenge has proven the easiest and Avatar quest has been used to great effect. The Avatar session was really weird which I enjoyed but I think some of the players were confused. Flashback worked in a pivotal case where a previous event was key to who the character was.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15603-phillycuriosity","name":"PhillyCuriosity","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15603_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}